Message-ID: <01FA499F-00C6-4363-A544-B2A047CF3731@gmail.com>

Hi all,

I have a question about the suppressor effect (?) in mixed-effect model. I am using lmer to run mixed-effect model on a sentence comprehension task. I am interested in the interactions between sentence comprehension performance and three independent variables (e.g. variable A, variable B, and variable C). When I run mixed-effect model with each independent variable alone, only variable A positively related to the sentence comprehension performance (e.g. t = 3). However, when I included all the three variables in the mixed-effects model simultaneously, the positive correlation between variable A and the sentence comprehension performance remained significant. But there is a negative correlation between variable B and the DV (e.g. t = -3), which is very unexpected. Based on the results from 
 some previous studies, even if there were a correlation between variable B and the DV, it should be a positive one. 

I am thinking about the possibility that variable B acted as a suppressor variable in the model, since taking out the effect of variable B, it increased the relation between variable A and the DV (as reflected by the t value). But when I run regression on the sentence comprehension performance (averaged across items) with all the three variables as predictors, the relation between variable B and the DV is far from significance. I am confused about how to explain such results. Has anyone met this problem before? 

I would appreciate if anyone could make any suggestion for how to interpret such results.

Many thanks,
Yingying

